# @@title@@

Made with `forge app new`. A product people can keep working on: a Python server (FastAPI,
SQLAlchemy 2, Alembic, PostgreSQL 16) with accounts, moderation and migrations, its tests, docs
and CI, and `forge.app.toml`, which tells Forge Web how to host it.

## Layout

| Path | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `server/app/` | the API: `main.py` builds the app; `auth.py`, `account.py`, `moderation.py` are its routes |
| `server/app/models.py` | the tables; change them together with a migration in `server/migrations/versions/` |
| `server/openapi.json` | the API description; the clients' types are generated from it |
| `server/tests/` | the server's tests (they need PostgreSQL, see below) |
| `docs/runbook.md` | running it: health, logs, backups, incidents |
| `docs/migrations.md` | changing the database without breaking a running release |
| `forge.app.toml` | what hosting needs: services, database, storage, mail, secret names |

## Run it locally

You need [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/) and a PostgreSQL 16 database (for example
`docker run -d -p 5432:5432 -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=dev postgres:16`).

```bash
cd server
uv sync
export DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:dev@localhost:5432/postgres
uv run alembic upgrade head
uv run uvicorn app.main:create_app --factory --reload --port 8000
```

The API is at http://localhost:8000/api and its interactive description at
http://localhost:8000/api/docs. Mail is written to the log until a mail relay is configured.

## Tests

The tests use a real PostgreSQL database and **empty it** first, so give them a database of their
own:

```bash
cd server
createdb app_test   # or: psql -c 'create database app_test'
TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:dev@localhost:5432/app_test uv run pytest
```

They check the API against `openapi.json` and the migrations against the models. After changing
the API, run `uv run python -m app.openapi_snapshot` and commit the new `openapi.json`. After
changing a model, add a migration: `uv run alembic revision --autogenerate -m "what changed"`,
read it, and follow `docs/migrations.md`.

## Hosting

Forge Web hosts the product from `forge.app.toml`, not from the Dockerfile (that one is for
people who want to run the server elsewhere). Hosting

- builds each service with its `build` command in a throwaway container and starts it with its
  `command`, with the service's `.venv/bin` first on `PATH`;
- sets `DATABASE_URL`, `PORT`, `APP_URL`, `STORAGE_DIR`, `MAIL_RELAY_URL` and `MAIL_RELAY_TOKEN`,
  and each secret listed under `secrets` (values are entered in Forge Web, never in this repo);
- checks `/healthz` before and after every release and rolls back when it fails;
- serves `/impressum`, `/datenschutz` and the "report content" link itself, with the operator's
  details entered in Forge Web.

Every release is reviewed and approved by you before it goes live.
